📖 WHAT IS THE IN OPERATOR?
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The IN operator is used in the WHERE clause to check whether a column's value matches
any value in a given list. It is a shorthand for multiple OR conditions and makes
queries shorter, easier to read, and simpler to maintain.

KEY BENEFITS:
• Shorter and more readable than repeated OR clauses
• Supports literal values and subqueries
• Works with numbers, strings, and expressions
• Can be negated with NOT IN


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
🔤 IN OPERATOR SYNTAX
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Basic syntax:
SELECT column_name(s)
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name IN (value1, value2, ...);

Negated form:
SELECT column_name(s)
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name NOT IN (value1, value2, ...);

Subquery form:
SELECT column_name(s)
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name IN (SELECT column_name FROM another_table WHERE ...);

NOT IN with subquery:
SELECT column_name(s)
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name NOT IN (SELECT column_name FROM another_table WHERE ...);


