🔁 IN OPERATOR WITH SUBQUERIES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The IN operator is frequently paired with subqueries to compare values across tables.
This is useful when you want to match rows from one table based on a lookup or result
set from another table.


1. SELECT CUSTOMERS WITH ORDERS USING IN (SELECT)
───────────────────────────────────────────────
QUERY:
SELECT * FROM Customers
WHERE CustomerID IN (SELECT CustomerID FROM Orders);

OUTPUT (Hypothetical Example):
┌────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────┐
│ CustomerID │ CustomerName                    │ ContactName      │ City         │
├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼──────────────┤
│ 1          │ Alfreds Futterkiste             │ Maria Anders     │ Berlin       │
│ 2          │ Ana Trujillo Emparedados        │ Ana Trujillo     │ México D.F.  │
│ 3          │ Antonio Moreno Taquería         │ Antonio Moreno   │ México D.F.  │
│ 4          │ Around the Horn                 │ Thomas Hardy     │ London       │
└────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┴──────────────┘

Rows affected: 4

EXPLANATION:
• Returns customers whose CustomerID appears in the Orders table
• This is useful for filtering only customers with orders


