💡 BEST PRACTICES FOR IN OPERATOR
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1. USE IN FOR MULTIPLE VALUES
   ─────────────────────────
   • Prefer IN over many OR clauses.
   • Example: WHERE Country IN ('Germany', 'UK', 'Sweden')
   • Equivalent OR version is longer and harder to maintain.

2. KEEP THE IN LIST SHORT WHEN POSSIBLE
   ────────────────────────────────────
   • A very large IN list can be hard to read and slower to parse.
   • For large sets, consider a temporary table or JOIN instead.

3. WATCH OUT FOR NULL VALUES WITH NOT IN
   ──────────────────────────────────
   • NOT IN behaves unexpectedly if the list contains NULL.
   • Example: WHERE Country NOT IN ('Germany', NULL)
   • This returns no rows, because NULL makes the comparison unknown.

4. USE SUBQUERIES FOR DYNAMIC LISTS
   ────────────────────────────────
   • IN (SELECT ...) is useful for values produced by another query.
   • Avoid subqueries when a JOIN would be more efficient.

5. COMPARE WITH EXIST
   ──────────────────
   • NOT IN (SELECT ...) can behave differently than NOT EXISTS.
   • If the subquery can return NULLs, NOT EXISTS is usually safer.

6. ORDER BY OR LIMIT AFTER IN
   ──────────────────────────
   • You can still sort and limit results when using IN.
   • Example: SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE Country IN (...) ORDER BY City;

7. USE IN WITH INDEXED COLUMNS
   ──────────────────────────
   • IN on indexed columns can be fast for small lists.
   • Use indexes for columns like CustomerID or Country if queries are common.


